A||||6||Tom88gt||Code 42 but runs ok?  ||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||05-10-2000||02:43 PM||||My car just started giving me a code 42.  Haynes only gives trouble shooting advise in the car isn't running, but the car runs fine, never a problem.  I've seen the other posts but I don't have red hot exhaust manifolds, at least not that I've noticed, or poor performance.  I have been having a recurring code 32, which I've been putting off but will probably eventually give in and spend the $$$ for the solenoid, that hasn't caused any noticable drivability problems either.<P>Any thoughts?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||05-10-2000||05:41 PM||||I ran across the link below a while back. It has some very useful info on trouble codes. Each code has a diagnostics section at the bottom that walks you thru by clicking yes/no answers at each step. I've never used it but it looks very well thought out. I guess you'll want your Fiero and computer as close together as possible though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A>  <P>Click on "Articles and Troubleshooting" then "V6 Trouble Code Guide".<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-10-2000||10:28 PM||||Actually what haynes syas holds for a running motor. Check over the distributor, pickup, and PIM. Make sure the PIM is mounted propperly. (Search for HOW TO: articles. There's one about the PIM mounting)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||05-11-2000||08:38 AM||||What's the PIM?  My distrubutor, cap, rotor, ignition pick up, ignition module, and coil are new.  ||||reg||5
Z||000004||theogre||05-11-2000||10:38 AM||||Primary Ignition Module. Most people leave off the word Primary. <P>Check the wires and don't count on the new PIM being good. If you still have the old one and it's functional put it back on and see if the problem goes away. Plus if the PIM wasn't mounted right it will over heat and screw up.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||05-11-2000||03:51 PM||||One day when you are bored, go into a GM parts department and have them print the revision list for the ignition modules. I figure they have to be at 15 or better. These things die regularly and GM keeps making revisions.<P>On the positive side, I've found the GM's tend to last longer than the aftermarkets especially when I've forgotten to put heatsink compound on them (not that I've ever really fogotten more then a couple of times).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||05-11-2000||04:58 PM||||I replaced the distributor a few months ago.  I got it from Auto Zone, includes the module, lifetime warranty, $99.  I guess I'll pull the distributor and have it checked and get a new one for free.||||reg||4
